Level Difficulty: Medium
Commands Used: ls,whoami,cat
              ls -> lists files, showed a setuid binary called uppershell
              whoami -> prints the current user
              cat -> reads and prints file content
Steps: ls [tried lowercase ls first and got "LS: not found" - the shell we landed in is uppercasing every command we type before running it, which is why "ls" became "LS" and failed]
       $0 [$0 normally holds the name/path of the currently running shell - typing it and running it spawns a fresh instance of that same shell as a new process, but this new instance is a normal shell, not the uppercasing wrapper, since $0 bypasses whatever logic was uppercasing our input]
       ls -la [now works normally in the fresh shell, shows uppershell - a setuid binary owned by bandit33 with group bandit32]
       whoami [confirms we're actually bandit33 now, not bandit32]
       cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit33 [read the password directly now that we're bandit33]

Note: the core trick here is that $0 sidesteps a shell that mangles your input (uppercasing every command).
